@@ -0,0 +1,352 @@
+Bounded supplied-stack pthreads, interface version 1
+==================================================
+
+Copyright (C) 2026 Dylan Plecki.
+This documentation is distributed under the GNU Lesser General Public
+License, version 2.1 or (at your option) any later version; see COPYING.LIB.
+
+This experimental additive interface implements a real allocator for selected
+NPTL and ELF TLS storage, with an explicitly selected strict execution mode.
+It is not a complete bounded profile for an arbitrary pthread entry point.
+Strict threads cannot use the ordinary allocator, resolver state, legacy RPC
+state or dynamic-loader operations.  User callbacks and application/library
+code still require an audited call closure and their own actual resource
+owners.  In particular, this implementation alone does not qualify a QEMU
+worker or its application allocations.
+
+The interface is declared in <pthread-bounded.h>.  It supports Linux/x86-64
+LP64 supplied stacks.  The four new symbols are pthread_bounded_init_np,
+pthread_bounded_create_np, pthread_bounded_query_np and
+pthread_bounded_tryretire_np, exported at GLIBC_2.39.  The configuration and
+receipt carry explicit version and size fields; version 1 rejects a different
+configuration version or layout.  This is a downstream ABI, not an upstream
+glibc API or a promise of compatibility with another implementation using the
+same symbol names.  The consumer must retain the matching headers, libc and
+ELF interpreter as a single runtime.  The private pthread descriptor grows to
+hold its owner pointer; its public pthread_t representation and ordinary
+pthread calling conventions do not change.  Mixing the patched libc with an
+old loader or old libthread_db is not qualified.
+
+Physical admission and custody
+------------------------------
+
+The caller supplies two distinct writable physical spans:
+
+* A guarded stack passed using pthread_attr_setstack.  The caller supplies
+  and retains the guards.  The supplied usable span already contains struct
+  pthread, static TLS, alignment padding and the first TSD block.  These bytes
+  are not charged a second time to the separate arena.  The actual
+  allocatestack geometry is recorded as supplied, reserved and available
+  stack bytes, including in a failed initial DTV allocation.
+* A containing arena passed to pthread_bounded_init_np.  Its beginning holds
+  the actual opaque control, immutable configuration and canonical factual
+  receipt.  Each allocation then consumes metadata, alignment padding and
+  payload inside that same span.  Metadata stores the actual owner, payload
+  size, allocation kind and logical-release flag.  It is not a copied credit.
+
+Each of the five kinds has an admitted request count, maximum payload bytes
+per request and maximum power-of-two alignment.  Init checks every arithmetic
+operation and reserves the sum of the control and each request's worst-case
+payload, metadata and alignment padding against actual arena bytes.  The
+allocator checks all three limits again at every request.  Neither exhaustion
+nor an alignment failure falls back to the ordinary heap.  Every allocation
+is zero-initialized, including the payload used for dynamic TLS.
+
+The implementation is deliberately monotonic.  Replacing a DTV consumes a new
+request; releasing its previous DTV records a logical release but does not
+make those bytes available for another request.  All padding, superseded
+blocks, released destructor nodes and unused reservation remain under the
+containing owner's custody until final terminal return.  There is no bounded
+stack cache.  A caller cannot use this interface's arena allocator for
+unrelated objects, transfer its nodes to another thread, copy its control,
+modify it, or release its backing while registered.
+
+Admission also consumes one of 128 actual fixed libc registry entries.  This
+process-level storage is separate from all per-thread arenas: on x86-64 the
+pointer array is 1024 bytes, with an additional 8-byte incarnation counter and
+4-byte trylock, plus actual compiler/linker alignment.  A complete enclosing
+profile must account for that process-level backing rather than invent a
+per-thread allowance that silently owns it.  Registry exhaustion returns
+EAGAIN.  The registry rejects a new arena overlapping any retained arena or
+supplied stack before writing control bytes.  Stack reservation rejects
+intersection with every retained arena and stack before allocating TLS or
+attempting clone.
+
+The configured x86-64 object currently places these in a 1056-byte .bss
+section with alignment 32: lock at offset 0, serial at offset 8, and pointer
+array at offset 32.  This includes 20 bytes of internal padding.  These are
+measured object-layout facts, not an allocator transfer or a complete process
+image charge.
+
+Init returns the canonical control address and a nonzero monotonically
+allocated incarnation.  Exhaustion of the 64-bit identity space returns
+EOVERFLOW; it never wraps.  Every subsequent operation requires both the
+actual control and the separately retained expected incarnation.  A registry
+lookup compares the nominated address before dereferencing it.  A stale
+incarnation at a reused address returns ESTALE.  A receipt copy, including a
+forged backing_returned flag, cannot register, reinitialize or release an
+owner.  These checks do not defend against arbitrary memory corruption within
+the calling process: the caller must honor opaque storage and exclusive
+lifetime custody, just as for other libc synchronization objects.
+
+Covered allocation and release paths
+------------------------------------
+
+The explicit bounded owner routes the following real allocation sites:
+
+* Initial DTV calloc, DTV growth/replacement, obsolete dynamic TLS release and
+  final DTV/dynamic TLS deallocation in elf/dl-tls.c.
+* Aligned dynamic TLS payload allocation reached through __tls_get_addr.
+* Second-level pthread_setspecific arrays and their exit-time releases.
+* __cxa_thread_atexit_impl destructor nodes and their actual post-callback
+  releases in __call_tls_dtors.
+* Priority-protection bookkeeping created by __pthread_tpp_change_priority
+  and released by the terminal provider.
+
+The first TSD array stays in the supplied TCB.  Static TLS stays on the
+supplied stack.  No heap allocation backs the bounded control or its
+per-request bookkeeping.  Ordinary threads have a NULL owner and continue to
+use their original allocation and stack-cache paths.  Ordinary join and
+detach reject an explicitly bounded thread with EINVAL; its containing owner
+must perform retirement.
+
+Initial DTV exhaustion returns creation failure before clone.  TSD exhaustion
+returns ENOMEM.  Bounded TLS destructor registration returns ENOMEM without
+publishing a destructor node.  Generated C++ callers may not handle such a
+failure usefully, so a complete supported caller must admit the real maximum
+number and size of its destructor registrations.  Unknown dynamic TLS module
+sizes, alignments or DTV growth are not harmless: the __tls_get_addr ABI has
+no error return.  Exceeding those admitted limits reaches glibc's existing
+fatal TLS OOM path, which exits the process with its existing diagnostic.
+There is no heap fallback.  A supported caller must preadmit the actual TLS
+module/request closure; an arbitrary future dlopen is not covered merely by
+having some numeric TLS allowance.
+
+Storage admission is separate from execution locks.  Destructor registration
+still takes dl_load_lock, finds the real DSO map and increments its actual
+l_tls_dtor_count.  On exit __call_tls_dtors invokes each callback before
+decrementing that map's reference count with release ordering and releasing
+the arena node.  The decrement itself takes no loader lock, but a callback
+can block, register another destructor, or touch dynamic TLS.  Allocating a
+destructor node therefore proves neither a bounded callback nor a nonwaiting
+exit tail.
+
+Initial TLS setup takes dl_load_tls_lock.  Dynamic TLS first access can also
+take that lock while deciding between static and forced-dynamic placement.
+_dl_update_slotinfo walks the process's actual module-slot list and can grow
+this thread's arena DTV.  _dl_add_to_slotinfo instead extends the process-wide
+loader slot list with ordinary malloc during module loading; that shared
+list is not charged to the thread arena.  Strict public loader operations
+and libc's internal dlopen/error wrappers refuse before entering those
+loader operations.  Another ordinary thread can still load modules: its
+global loader storage needs its own actual containing process owner, while
+the bounded thread pays only its actual DTV and dynamic TLS requests.  The
+growth test exercises that distinction and does not qualify a complete
+module-loading profile.  Terminal tryretire only deallocates the already
+bounded thread's nodes; it does not execute callbacks or grow slotinfo.
+
+Creation and terminal facts
+---------------------------
+
+One creation attempt consumes an initialized control, including a validation
+or no-birth failure.  The public receipt separates create_attempted,
+clone_attempted, born, user-entry dispatch and actual exit-cleanup completion.
+A clone followed by failed affinity or scheduler setup remains a retained,
+joinable, never-entered birth.  The creating operation releases it to perform
+its existing early kernel exit and returns the creation error without joining
+or freeing it.  Its real thread-count adjustment remains pending until the
+kernel exit is observed.  Ordinary pthread_create retains its original
+blocking partial-failure cleanup.
+
+The caller must retain its own semantic operation permission.  No libc
+thread count, ID, incarnation, receipt or successful resource return grants
+permission to perform another operation.
+
+Tryretire attempts each internal lock once.  It never waits on a futex,
+spins behind a busy lock, calls the ordinary stack-cache retirement path or
+returns memory to the ordinary heap.  It first observes the kernel's
+clear-child-tid store with acquire ordering.  Only then can it mark the actual
+kernel join.  A completed entry point or last destructor alone is insufficient:
+rseq and other kernel references can remain live until kernel exit.
+
+After the join, a busy real loader stack-list/cache lock returns EBUSY with
+joined and kernel_exited preserved and list/TLS/backing custody retained.  A
+later call can remove the exact supplied-stack list member and return its
+bounded TLS nodes.  A normal successful birth whose actual libc destructor
+and freeres tail did not complete retains EPROTO debt even if its kernel tid
+is clear.  A raw SYS_exit must not be mistaken for libc cleanup.  The known
+born-never-entered setup-failure path is handled separately.
+
+The provider checks that all actual arena allocations have corresponding
+logical releases, then actually releases its registry entry and returns stack
+and arena custody.  It does not munmap the caller's spans.  The external final
+receipt records stack_returned, registration_returned and backing_returned;
+only this completed terminal operation permits the containing caller to
+return those actual spans.  All receipt and result buffers must reside
+outside both spans.  A copied successful receipt cannot return another
+incarnation.  After final return the old control is no longer queryable;
+the external receipt is the retained history.
+
+Every writable output is checked against all canonical retained arenas and
+supplied stacks under the registry trylock at the actual write.  Creation
+uses a local pthread_t until publishing its output.  If another admission
+occupies an output span during creation, publication refuses and canonical
+birth/custody facts remain queryable; it does not undo a real birth.  Output
+publication itself may return EBUSY without a new external snapshot.  The
+caller retains its previous history and can query the canonical owner again.
+
+The first error is sticky and independent of current retirement status and
+physical facts.  A later successful return can truthfully coexist with an
+earlier EBUSY or independent negative operation.  A caller's own negative
+post or unknown cleanup does not erase known resource consumption and cannot
+justify reusing uncertain live storage.
+
+Actual exit-tail audit and limits
+---------------------------------
+
+pthread_create runs __call_tls_dtors, __nptl_deallocate_tsd and
+__libc_thread_freeres before normal kernel exit.  The completion flag is stored
+only after all three actually return.  User callbacks can block, allocate or
+exit abnormally; tryretire reports retained debt while their kernel thread
+remains live and does not claim the callback's external resources are bounded.
+
+Version 1 requires PTHREAD_BOUNDED_STRICT in config.flags.  It enforces
+these additional restrictions before the corresponding ordinary producer:
+
+* malloc, calloc, realloc, aligned allocation and allocator management refuse
+  before ptmalloc initialization, tcache, arena selection or global locks.
+  Pointer-returning allocations return NULL with errno ENOTSUP;
+  posix_memalign returns ENOTSUP without modifying its output.  Management
+  functions return their failure/empty result with errno ENOTSUP.
+* free(NULL) remains a no-op.  Any nonnull free refuses before inspecting the
+  pointer or entering the allocator.  It records the first refused pointer
+  and sticky foreign-free debt.  Because free has no error result, the
+  receipt is the explicit failure channel; errno is also ENOTSUP.  This
+  retained debt prevents backing return even after a successful kernel join.
+  The provider never claims that the foreign allocation was consumed.
+* Resolver initialization/context acquisition and explicit resolver close
+  refuse before ordinary allocations, configuration references or socket
+  effects.  They report the ABI's failure result and errno ENOTSUP.
+* strsignal returns NULL with errno ENOTSUP.  strerror_l returns a static
+  unsupported-operation diagnostic with errno ENOTSUP, before translation
+  or buffer production.  dlerror similarly returns a static diagnostic;
+  _dlerror_run refuses before loader operations and error allocation.
+* Legacy RPC variable access has no usable operation error ABI.  It records
+  refusal and terminates the process with exit_group(127), before once
+  initialization, allocation, or access to fabricated shared state.
+
+The allocator_refusals and foreign_free_refusals counters are saturated
+monotonic lower bounds.  Each recording operation attempts one strong CAS;
+if it loses, a competing increment already supplied a positive lower bound.
+There is no retry loop, wrap, allocator call or lock fallback.  Concurrent or
+reentrant refusals can coalesce.  Sticky first_error and first_refused_free
+are independently recorded with one strong CAS.
+
+The real freeres routines still run.  Before resolver/RPC/error-buffer or
+allocator cleanup can enter an ordinary allocator or global-state cleanup,
+they inspect the actual per-thread state.  Unexpected nonempty state sets
+cleanup_blocked and retains it without pretending to release it.  The normal
+exit-cleanup completion flag is then withheld, and retirement retains EPROTO
+debt after the factual kernel join.  A supported strict thread has no such
+state: tcache and thread_arena remain NULL, resolver and RPC state remain
+empty, and TLS/error buffers remain NULL.  The no-state cleanup path reaches
+no ordinary allocator or allocator global lock.
+
+Invalid, foreign or duplicate arena-node release also sets sticky
+cleanup_blocked debt.  Equal allocated/released counters alone cannot return
+backing after an uncertain TLS pointer release.  Terminal code withholds
+tls_returned if the actual TLS deallocation encountered that debt, while
+preserving earlier factual list removal and individual logical releases.
+
+This is scoped libc thread/TLS/control enforcement.  It does not intercept
+arbitrary mmap, file/socket operations, user allocators, external libraries,
+or every unrelated libc global-state API.  Application allocations needed by
+a caller require a distinct genuine preadmitted allocator owner and return
+protocol or existing paid storage.  They cannot be relabeled as a TLS/control
+allowance.  Actual caller compatibility must be audited; copied limits and
+successful bounded storage tests cannot establish that call closure.
+
+Debug thread-event reporting is refused before bounded creation.  Debugger
+mutation of descriptors, namespace duplication of libc, cross-thread use of
+arena nodes, asynchronous signal-handler allocation and interposed thread/TLS
+implementations are not qualified.  The API supplies nonwaiting *retirement*,
+not a latency guarantee for creation, TLS lookup, user callbacks or thread
+exit.  Creation still uses the supplied-stack list lock, TLS loader locks,
+first-thread late_init and stdio lock initialization, and any stopped-start
+scheduling handoff.  It can therefore wait; no whole-operation nonwaiting
+creation claim is made.  The nonwaiting guarantees apply to registry/init,
+query and terminal tryretire lock cuts.  Ordinary pthread behavior remains
+unchanged.
+
+Causal controls
+---------------
+
+tst-pthread-bounded uses a genuinely guarded caller mapping and separate
+physical arena.  It checks initial-DTV exhaustion with no clone; a real
+empty-affinity post-clone failure that never enters user code; enforced TSD
+and destructor request exhaustion; actual priority-control storage; alignment;
+actual process slot-list growth and thread DTV growth using 80 distinct loaded
+DSOs; last-destructor retention;
+actual held stack-list/cache lock refusal after a factual kernel join; sticky
+negative history; canonical registration despite a modified receipt; rejection
+of an arena overlapping live worker-local bytes; supplied-stack alias refusal;
+exact per-kind final logical release; same-backing reuse with a new
+incarnation; stale-incarnation refusal; and fatal dynamic TLS exhaustion
+rather than heap fallback; strict allocator/resolver/error refusals;
+foreign-free retained debt; unexpected exit-tail state; raw kernel exit;
+and independent-owner output alias rejection preserving live stack bytes.
+These are implementation controls, not a complete
+application profile or proof for an unexecuted platform.
+
+Qualification on Linux/x86-64 LP64
+----------------------------------
+
+A standard full library build completed with GNU GCC 16.2.0, binutils 2.41,
+GNU make 4.4 and source-built prerequisites, with private regular source and
+output copies.  Compilation used one compiler lane and an inherited 2 GiB
+per-process address-space ceiling.  The largest recorded compiler-child peak
+resident set was 135168 KiB over 16465 recorded driver invocations, including
+failed and incremental builds and test compilation.  This is a measured child
+peak, not an aggregate process-tree memory guarantee.  The first successful
+full build took approximately 14 minutes 22 seconds.  Later full builds
+included the final strict enforcement and initial-DTV failure fix.
+
+The actual tst-pthread-bounded test reports PASS with original exit status 0.
+Its exhaustion and retained-debt cases exercise the provider, rather than a
+mock allocator.  The measured x86-64 sizes are: opaque owner 544 bytes,
+allocation metadata 24 bytes, configuration 136 bytes, receipt 368 bytes and
+struct pthread 2432 bytes.  All sizes and ABI qualifications are specific to
+this configured build.  The control test uses -fno-builtin so allocator calls
+whose refusal is asserted remain actual calls; a prior optimized test that
+removed those calls was retained as a failed control, not counted as PASS.
+
+Eleven targeted ordinary tests also report PASS: tst-basic1, tst-join1,
+tst-join2, tst-key1, tst-key3, tst-tls3 and tst-attr3 in nptl; and tst-malloc,
+tst-calloc, tst-memalign and tst-malloc-thread-exit in malloc.  The join and
+cancellation tests require a genuine source-built libgcc_s.so.1, explicitly
+added after the freshly built libc directories in the test interpreter's
+library path.  An earlier tst-join1 failure without that runtime was retained
+as a missing-runtime prerequisite failure.  These ordinary tests do not
+qualify bounded pthread_exit or cancellation: their unwind/runtime loader
+closure needs a separate audit, and strict internal loader entry refuses
+before an unsupported lazy load.  Interposed allocators and foreign unwinding
+implementations are outside this qualification.
+
+The complete check-abi invocation reports failure: 19 individual ABI checks
+pass, and check-abi-libc differs only in _nl_default_dirname data size, 0x12
+versus 0x3d.  The configured private install prefix makes LOCALEDIR longer;
+all other libc entries, including the four additive GLIBC_2.39 symbols,
+match.  The upstream default-prefix ABI entry was deliberately not rewritten
+to conceal that configured-prefix difference.  No complete check-abi PASS,
+full testsuite PASS, different-platform qualification or complete caller
+profile is claimed.
+
+Initial build and test failures were retained before fixes: private source
+executable-mode restoration, Python resource-module availability, generated
+build prerequisites, header identifiers and atomic primitives, stack pointer
+arithmetic, malloc-debug source inclusion, control-test interpreter-global
+lookup, rtld-private errno on failed initial DTV allocation, and optimized
+away test allocations.  In particular, the bounded initial-DTV failure now
+returns ENOMEM before the ordinary path's libc-errno assertion; the ordinary
+path retains its original assertion and behavior.  Successful later runs do
+not erase those first failures.
